          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I am 52 and my LDL cholesterol is 165 mg/dL. Does that number alone decide treatment, what risk bands does guidance use, and which modifiable coronary-risk factors does the corpus describe?",
          "answer": "[removed: this system does not give doses or amounts.] The main lipid guidance says treatment is driven by your estimated 10-year atherosclerotic cardiovascular risk, \"not by the LDL number alone\" (*Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation*). That estimate depends on several inputs, including age, sex, blood pressure, diabetes, and smoking. The corpus does not give a fixed LDL number that triggers treatment, so your clinician will need to calculate your overall risk with you.\n\nOn risk bands, the sources disagree about what the corpus contains. One review reports that the lipid document sets four 10-year bands for adults aged 40 to 75 without established heart disease: low (below 5%), borderline (5% to below 7.5%), intermediate (7.5% to below 20%), and high (20% or above). The other review's retrieved passages did not include numeric bands or LDL cut-offs. I cannot confirm the bands from the passages I have, so ask your clinician which risk calculator and thresholds they use. The lipid document also reportedly notes that United States guidance frames treatment around statin intensity and percentage LDL reduction, while European guidance sets explicit LDL goals that fall as risk rises. The corpus does not say which approach is better.\n\nThe *Coronary artery disease* reference lists these modifiable risk factors: smoking, high blood pressure, raised LDL cholesterol, diabetes, obesity, physical inactivity, a diet high in saturated fat and low in vegetables and fiber, chronic kidney disease, and inflammatory conditions. It also names family history of premature coronary disease and says age and male sex cannot be changed. It does not clearly say whether family history is modifiable, so ask your clinician. The retrieved passages do not explain how each factor changes LDL levels. For general measures, the hypertension guidance lists a diet rich in vegetables, fruit and whole grains and low in saturated fat, regular aerobic activity, weight reduction where needed, limiting alcohol, and stopping smoking. These are not LDL-specific.\n\nThis is general information, not a diagnosis. Please discuss your LDL result and your overall cardiovascular risk with your clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
